WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.307 --> 00:00:03.140
(loud explosions)

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.300
- The big ship is not stuck
anymore, which is kinda cool.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.590
There was this big cargo ship
is like a quarter mile long

00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:16.070
and it was stuck in the Suez Canal,

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:17.740
created this huge traffic jam.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:18.700
- [News Reporter] Blocking
one of the world's

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:20.500
most important shipping lines.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:22.360
- They spent days trying to dig it out.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:25.040
It was costing like $400 million an hour

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:27.460
for the global economy,
and now it's not stuck.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:31.230
But I can't stop thinking
about it because this ship

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:33.210
and it being stuck for a few days

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:36.550
reminds us just what the global economy is

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:40.330
and how important one
little strip of water can be

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:41.460
both for the economy,

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.823
but also for countries who want power.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:48.030
So, I'm gonna tell you an
insane story about this canal.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:50.650
It was the 1960s and the US government

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:55.130
was thinking about using 520 nuclear bombs

00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:57.740
to make a new canal for themselves

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:00.763
right here next to the Suez Canal.

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:04.760
We're talking over a hundred
kilometers of a new canal

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:06.250
to connect the Mediterranean

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:09.153
to the Red Sea using nuclear bombs.

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:12.893
Wait, what?

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:15.810
Oh my gosh, it's so insane.

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:17.270
Like it's so insane.

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:20.410
This was gonna cost like $5
billion in today's terms.

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:21.770
And the idea here was to create

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:23.900
a strategically valuable alternate

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:25.890
to the present day Suez Canal.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:27.400
To understand why the US

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:30.800
would even consider blowing up 520 nukes

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:32.010
in the Israeli desert.

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:35.080
You have to understand
this little strip of water

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:37.140
and why it has been so vital

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.600
to so many powerful
countries over the years.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.413
First, let's look at a map.

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:45.630
This is an amazing
project from shipmap.org,

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:48.550
where each dot represents a cargo ship,

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:50.760
similar size to the Empire State Building.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:51.600
Each of these ships

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.850
is carrying around 1,000 containers.

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:58.024
It's like 25,000 tons of stuff.

00:01:58.024 --> 00:02:00.607
(upbeat music)

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:06.960
These ships fuel our lifestyles.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.850
A good example is iPhone if you have one.

00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:11.480
Before you ever see your iPhone,

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:13.500
there's an amazing
journey that it goes on.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.910
Every individual part of your iPhone

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:17.340
has its own little story.

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:20.090
They're sourced from
43 different countries

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:21.510
on all six continents.

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:23.670
There's no way I could go
into every single component,

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:25.310
but let's just take one
thing, the processor,

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:27.510
the sort of brain of your iPhone.

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:31.610
The processor starts as raw
materials, tons of raw materials

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:34.690
like boron from Turkey
or copper from Chile

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:37.660
or tantalum from Rwanda and way, way more

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:39.220
that are mined in different countries

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:43.030
and shipped to a factory in
Taiwan where it is manufactured

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:44.360
and then shipped to the Philippines

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:47.470
to be tested and packaged before
it is then shipped to China

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:49.600
to be integrated into your phone.

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:52.270
And that is just for the little
processor in your iPhone,

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:55.700
not to mention the screen and
the casing and the camera.

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:57.650
Each one of these has components

00:02:57.650 --> 00:02:59.690
that are sourced from all around world.

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:01.150
They go through this complex journey

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:02.280
to then be assembled

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:04.750
and then get put back
onto a shipping container

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:07.670
to be shipped to your
country where you can buy it.

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:08.890
No matter how I put it,

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:11.770
saying it's a complex global supply chain,

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:13.990
there's no way we can even fathom

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:17.180
just how big and massive
these operations are.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:19.100
The amount of space of ocean

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:21.080
that these products pass through

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:23.110
to eventually make it into our hands.

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:24.120
And this is everything,

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:26.550
my clothing, my computer,
my camera equipment,

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:29.060
everything around me
passes through this system.

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:31.320
To add another layer of weird complexity,

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.720
the ships themselves
like the physical ships

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.030
are also mind bendingly complex.

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:37.270
So let's take the ever given,

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:40.420
this ship that was stuck
in the Suez Canal recently.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:44.440
This ship is chartered
by a Taiwanese company.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:46.370
But it is registered in Panama.

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:47.700
I've talked about ship registration

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:49.140
in a different video by the way,

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:51.530
and why ships wanna be registered
in different countries.

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:52.980
Anyway, it is registered in Panama

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:54.490
charted by a Taiwanese company,

00:03:54.490 --> 00:03:56.470
but the ship itself, the physical ship

00:03:56.470 --> 00:03:58.690
is owned by a Japanese company

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:02.370
which is owned by a bigger
conglomerate of ship builders.

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:04.120
Oh, and the technical manager on board

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:05.440
is actually a German guy,

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:08.530
and the crew are a punch
of Indian nationals.

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:11.900
I mean, this ship itself is just a symbol

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:14.950
of how big and convoluted and multilayered

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:17.540
this huge global system is.

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:18.930
Okay, so that's the global economy.

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:21.310
Let's get back to the Suez Canal.

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:23.150
Take a look at this canal
for like five seconds

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:24.880
and you realize that it's not natural,

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:26.180
way to straight of lines.

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:27.440
But back before it was built,

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:29.700
if you wanted to get from Europe to Asia,

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:31.555
you had to sort of do this.

00:04:31.555 --> 00:04:34.850
(upbeat music)

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:36.030
Back then the British controlled

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:37.610
this little port in South Africa

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:39.980
which gave them a very
nice strategic advantage

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:42.370
because everyone who was doing world trade

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:44.810
or domination had to pass through them.

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:46.970
The British were loving this monopoly

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:50.170
over the trade route
until like the mid 1800s

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:51.900
when the French went to the Egyptians

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:53.226
and they said,

00:04:53.226 --> 00:04:54.770
"Hey, is there any way we
could like dig out a canal

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:56.310
right here on this land bridge

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:58.300
that connects Africa with Asia?

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:00.650
It's almost 200 kilometers long,

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:03.100
but if we could just dig out a canal here,

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:07.040
we would save thousands of
kilometers on each trip to Asia."

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:09.185
Reminder, this is like the 1860s

00:05:09.185 --> 00:05:10.770
and like, they don't have loads

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:14.193
of fancy equipment to do
this, but they try it anyway.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:18.100
With over 100,000 people,

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:19.950
many of them forced the laborers

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:21.790
working on this canal for over 10 years,

00:05:21.790 --> 00:05:24.010
they were able to dig this big canal

00:05:24.010 --> 00:05:27.133
that connected the Mediterranean
Sea to the Red Sea.

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:29.947
The British were down here still like,

00:05:29.947 --> 00:05:32.360
"Wait, why is no one coming
through our Cape anymore?"

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:33.520
And the French were up here,

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:37.486
just laughing, feeling so
good about their new canal.

00:05:37.486 --> 00:05:39.860
(laughing)

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:41.920
So over the years, quickly this becomes

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:45.540
the epicenter of water
control and balance of power.

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:48.230
Control over the canal is
mainly French and Egyptian

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:49.490
for most of its time.

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:50.380
But eventually the British,

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:51.660
they couldn't help themselves,

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:53.720
they needed in on this waterway.

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:54.800
Fast forward a few years

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:56.410
and all of the big powers get together

00:05:56.410 --> 00:05:58.170
and say like, "Whoa, whoa,
whoa we built this canal

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:01.200
with a spirit of
cooperation and neutrality.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:04.090
All countries should be
able to use this canal

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:07.180
even in times of war,
everything will be peaceful

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:08.970
and wonderful and this will be one."

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:10.530
No, no, no, no, no.

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:12.580
They signed this treaty
about like neutrality

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:14.770
and everyone can use it, but
let's just say it right now,

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:16.520
that's not the way the world works.

00:06:18.381 --> 00:06:21.150
Soon, the globe descended into World War

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:23.040
and it became clear that this canal

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:25.250
would not be neutral anytime soon.

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:28.250
Hitler wanted it, so him
and his Italian allies

00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:30.507
went down to try to take it
over, British fought back

00:06:30.507 --> 00:06:31.880
and they were able to keep it

00:06:31.880 --> 00:06:33.480
and there were British troops there.

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:35.790
So the canal remained in British control

00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:37.270
until like the 1950s.

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:38.797
When finally Egypt was like,

00:06:38.797 --> 00:06:41.580
"Okay listen, France,
Britain, this has been fun.

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:43.420
This whole pretend that this is neutral,

00:06:43.420 --> 00:06:44.480
but it's not actually neutral

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:45.540
and you're actually just using it

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:46.690
for whatever you want thing.

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:49.470
You're done, it is our
canal, get out of here."

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:52.950
And he's like, "Oh, and
Israel you can't come here

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:54.690
and use our waterway either.

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:55.720
That's part of this."

00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:57.990
So France and Britain
aren't feeling super great.

00:06:57.990 --> 00:06:59.130
They just got kicked out

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:01.810
of like their key strategic waterway.

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:03.530
They decide to say no

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:06.550
and they send in a bunch of
paratroopers to the Suez Canal,

00:07:06.550 --> 00:07:08.230
along with Israel's fighters

00:07:08.230 --> 00:07:10.610
to recapture the canal from Egypt

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:14.413
and to overthrow the government
that just kicked them out.

00:07:14.413 --> 00:07:17.500
(upbeat music)

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:18.830
And the US has seen all of this

00:07:18.830 --> 00:07:21.890
and they're like, "Guys,
it's not 1850 anymore.

00:07:21.890 --> 00:07:22.890
Times have changed.

00:07:22.890 --> 00:07:24.590
You can't just show up wherever you want

00:07:24.590 --> 00:07:26.490
and just take it over militarily.

00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:27.410
We don't do that anymore.

00:07:27.410 --> 00:07:29.260
That's so like old school empire."

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:31.860
But the US says, "You
can still be an empire

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:32.710
in these times,

00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:35.329
you just have to be a
little smoother about it."

00:07:35.329 --> 00:07:36.162
♪ Go baby go ♪

00:07:36.162 --> 00:07:37.900
- So, US sends in this Canadian guy

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:39.800
to settle the conflict over the canal.

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:41.610
And everyone's sort of like, "Okay, fine."

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:43.580
And the canal goes back to peace

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:47.280
with like some UN peacekeepers
to keep it at peace kind of.

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:48.660
Well, no, this isn't going to last.

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:50.580
So this is where the US gets it creative

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:51.890
and it's like, "Ooh, what can we do

00:07:51.890 --> 00:07:53.770
to not have to fight over the canal,

00:07:53.770 --> 00:07:54.840
but like do our own thing."

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:57.030
And that's when the nuclear bombs come up.

00:07:57.030 --> 00:07:58.288
Where they're like,

00:07:58.288 --> 00:08:00.120
"If we just plant 520 nuclear bombs

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:01.240
here in the Israeli desert,

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:02.390
we could just have our own canal

00:08:02.390 --> 00:08:04.617
and then we don't have
to fight over this one."

00:08:04.617 --> 00:08:07.367
(blasting sound)

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:11.377
So, it's 1967 and Israel's like,

00:08:11.377 --> 00:08:14.310
"I'm not super into the idea
of you like nuking our desert,

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:16.090
but we like nuclear weapons

00:08:16.090 --> 00:08:17.790
and we'll totally get on board with that."

00:08:17.790 --> 00:08:21.340
In the meantime, can we go
in and attack Egypt ourselves

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:22.173
and get the canal

00:08:22.173 --> 00:08:24.000
and you can just sort of
back us from the sidelines.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:26.214
And the US is like, "Okay, fine."

00:08:26.214 --> 00:08:28.797
(upbeat music)

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:35.340
So Israel goes in and
takes over all of this land

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:37.530
all the way up to the Suez Canal.

00:08:37.530 --> 00:08:40.270
So it's Egypt on one
side Israel on the other.

00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:41.760
And for the next few years,

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:45.980
this becomes a literal battle
ground for multiple wars.

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:48.360
Like they were building
bridges across the canal

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:50.597
to like bring in tanks and fight.

00:08:50.597 --> 00:08:53.360
And of course, during
these years of fighting

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:55.780
from like the late 60s to the mid 70s,

00:08:55.780 --> 00:08:58.330
the canal was closed for obvious reasons.

00:08:58.330 --> 00:08:59.730
The problem is when they closed it,

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:01.610
there were 15 ships that were like

00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:03.470
in process of moving through

00:09:03.470 --> 00:09:05.200
and those ships just got stuck there

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:06.850
for literally eight years.

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:10.430
So yeah, ever given, we know
that it was a hard few days

00:09:10.430 --> 00:09:12.307
who the world was super worried about you,

00:09:12.307 --> 00:09:13.430
$400 million a day.

00:09:13.430 --> 00:09:16.920
Imagine being one of
these guys, eight years.

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:20.120
At least they had fun
during their time, I guess.

00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:21.910
Do you see where I'm going with this?

00:09:21.910 --> 00:09:22.830
What I'm trying to do here

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:25.470
is not give you a history
lesson of the Suez Canal.

00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:28.300
I'm trying to explain
that this stretch of water

00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:31.740
has always been a magnet
for international conflict.

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:34.150
ever since it's very, very conception.

00:09:34.150 --> 00:09:35.690
After that last war,

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:38.290
the US is like, okay, broker our peace,

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:39.280
they broke our piece.

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:42.220
And then they bring in these
helicopters to the Suez Canal

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:44.160
with like a water sled behind them

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:46.350
and they drag them along the water

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:47.360
because there was a bunch

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:49.467
of like live explosives from the waters.

00:09:49.467 --> 00:09:50.840
And they were trying to like rid them all

00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:53.660
so that they could presume
like normal shipping lanes.

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:54.590
They eventually did that.

00:09:54.590 --> 00:09:55.950
They got rid of all the explosives

00:09:55.950 --> 00:09:58.680
and reopened the canal in 1975.

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:00.440
And the canal remained peaceful

00:10:00.440 --> 00:10:02.610
and under the control
of its rightful owner,

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:05.670
the Egyptians, forevermore,
and there was no more conflict

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:07.973
or geopolitical scheming again.

00:10:10.451 --> 00:10:12.670
No, that isn't the way the world works.

00:10:12.670 --> 00:10:15.290
Yes, things have changed
in the way that we do it.

00:10:15.290 --> 00:10:18.730
But the fact is, when you
have a system like this

00:10:18.730 --> 00:10:21.500
and a large portion of that system

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:24.950
which is money and control
and economics and energy

00:10:24.950 --> 00:10:28.160
runs through a choke point in that system,

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:31.300
there will always be geostrategic value

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:32.490
that is put on that system.

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:35.100
Not just for economics, but for power.

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:36.980
So do you think that the United States

00:10:36.980 --> 00:10:39.360
who's sort of the big boy
world policeman right now

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:43.080
would sit here and let
Egypt control the choke hold

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:45.320
where 12% of the world
economy passes through

00:10:45.320 --> 00:10:46.520
including loads of oil.

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:47.720
No, that's not how it works.

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:50.810
Despite the fact that Egypt
is a nondemocratic regime,

00:10:50.810 --> 00:10:51.760
but it's pretty terrible

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:53.610
on all fronts including human rights.

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:58.460
The US still provides $1.4
billion of military aid

00:10:58.460 --> 00:10:59.600
to Egypt every year.

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:03.570
They are always considered a
key ally in the middle East.

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:06.010
The Clinton administration
made it very clear

00:11:06.010 --> 00:11:08.320
that the reason we support Egypt

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:10.900
is because they have the Suez Canal

00:11:10.900 --> 00:11:12.930
and we need them to owe favors.

00:11:12.930 --> 00:11:14.280
That continues today.

00:11:14.280 --> 00:11:17.000
Whenever a US military vessel
shows up to the Suez Canal,

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:17.980
they get to cut in line

00:11:17.980 --> 00:11:19.920
and go through quicker
than everybody else.

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:22.100
So, yeah, Egypt owns the canal,

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:24.090
but really it's the us behind the scenes

00:11:24.090 --> 00:11:26.590
that is keeping everything in order.

00:11:26.590 --> 00:11:27.900
The canal is not neutral.

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:29.150
It will never be neutral.

00:11:29.150 --> 00:11:32.770
There will always be geopolitical
importance placed on it.

00:11:32.770 --> 00:11:34.400
And as long as there's a superpower,

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:36.663
that power will control the canal.

00:11:36.663 --> 00:11:39.420
(upbeat music)

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:42.170
The last thing I'll say
is that we live in a world

00:11:42.170 --> 00:11:45.570
that is very strange
for how peaceful it is.

00:11:45.570 --> 00:11:48.550
We've had an enduring peace
for many, many decades.

00:11:48.550 --> 00:11:52.253
That piece allows us to
have a system like this.

00:11:53.610 --> 00:11:56.300
And that piece exists partly because

00:11:56.300 --> 00:11:58.900
we are so entangled with
one another economically

00:11:58.900 --> 00:12:00.610
that it doesn't make sense to go to war,

00:12:00.610 --> 00:12:02.950
but we have to remember
that that piece also exists

00:12:02.950 --> 00:12:05.730
because all the big powers
have really big weapons

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:07.700
that are always pointed at each other.

00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:10.890
If something slips or
escalates a little too far,

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:12.370
just to remember that the Suez Canal

00:12:12.370 --> 00:12:14.450
isn't the only choke point on earth

00:12:14.450 --> 00:12:16.280
where all of this system flows through

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:18.300
and that while we take
peace for granted now,

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:20.940
it would be naive not
to wonder what happens

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:22.690
if that were to go away

00:12:22.690 --> 00:12:23.523
and we were faced

00:12:23.523 --> 00:12:25.910
with not just one ship
being stuck for a few days,

00:12:25.910 --> 00:12:28.420
but an entire region militarized

00:12:28.420 --> 00:12:31.230
and unavailable for this
type of shipping flow

00:12:31.230 --> 00:12:32.290
that we're so used to.

00:12:32.290 --> 00:12:34.580
And for years, I don't
wanna sound alarmist,

00:12:34.580 --> 00:12:36.440
but I do want us to be thinking

00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:38.530
about our reliance on the system

00:12:38.530 --> 00:12:42.320
and what it means for the
future of rising powers

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:46.370
that may not want to cooperate
as well as they do today.

00:12:46.370 --> 00:12:49.490
So when I see ever given blown
off course for a few days

00:12:49.490 --> 00:12:50.640
and everyone freaking out about it,

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:52.010
I don't just see one ship

00:12:52.010 --> 00:12:54.270
I see a vulnerable system

00:12:54.270 --> 00:12:57.210
and I see myself and how deeply connected

00:12:57.210 --> 00:12:59.263
I am to that system.

00:12:59.263 --> 00:13:01.846
